{% comment %}
  Renders one metafield as finished page content, whatever type it is.

  Every dynamic part of the product page needs the same thing: take a metafield
  a merchant filled in and put it on the page correctly. Doing that per caller
  is what produced the bugs this snippet exists to end - a list metafield
  rendered as one run-on string, a file metafield rendered as the literal word
  its drop stringifies to, a rich text metafield escaped into visible tags.

  So the type switch lives here, once. Callers resolve *which* metafield they
  want; this decides whether that is a paragraph, a bulleted list, an image, a
  video or a link.

  Pass the metafield itself, never its `.value`. The type is only readable on
  the metafield, and without the type every value is an indistinguishable
  string - which is exactly how the old per-caller code went wrong.

  Types handled:
    rich_text_field                         its own HTML, as authored
    single_line_text_field                  <p>, line breaks preserved
    multi_line_text_field                   <p>, line breaks preserved
    file_reference        image             responsive <img>
                          video             <video>, muted loop, no controls
                          anything else     a download link
    page_reference                          that page's content
    url                                     a link
    boolean                                 "Yes" / "No"
    number_integer / number_decimal         the number
    dimension / weight / volume             value + unit, e.g. "42 in"
    rating                                  value + scale, e.g. "4.5 / 5"
    money                                   formatted in the shop's currency
    date / date_time                        formatted long, e.g. "4 March 2026"
    color                                   a swatch chip beside the hex
    product / collection / variant ref      a link to it
    list.<any of the above>                 a <ul>, or a stack for files

  Not handled: metaobject_reference, json. Liquid forbids a snippet rendering
  itself, so a metaobject - whose fields are themselves metafields of arbitrary
  type - cannot be walked from here without duplicating this whole switch. Both
  render nothing rather than something wrong; callers that need a metaobject
  read its fields themselves.

  Accepts:
  - field: {Object} the metafield, e.g. product.metafields.custom.fabric.
  - bare:  {Boolean} omit the <p> around plain text, for callers that already
            have a text container. Default false.

  Usage:
  {% render 'pdp-metafield', field: product.metafields.custom.fabric %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign f = field
  assign t = f.type | default: ''

  comment
    A list metafield is the scalar type with 'list.' in front, and its value is
    an array. Strip the prefix so one `case` serves both, and remember which it
    was so the markup can differ - a list of sizes wants bullets, a single
    paragraph does not.
  endcomment
  assign is_list = false
  assign base = t
  if t contains 'list.'
    assign is_list = true
    assign base = t | remove_first: 'list.'
  endif

  assign date_format = '%-d %B %Y'
-%}

{%- case base -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Rich text arrives as HTML Shopify built from the editor's own structure, so
    it is output as-is. Escaping it here would put visible <p> tags on the page;
    `newline_to_br` on top of it would double every paragraph break.

    A list of rich text has no wrapper: each entry is already block-level
    content, and a <ul> of paragraphs indents copy that was never meant to be
    indented.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'rich_text_field' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      {%- for item in f.value -%}{{ item }}{%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ f.value }}
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Plain text. Escaped because a merchant pasting "M & L" or "<40cm" into a
    text field means those characters literally, then `newline_to_br` so the
    line breaks they typed survive into HTML. That order matters: escaping
    after would turn the <br> tags back into visible text.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'single_line_text_field' or 'multi_line_text_field' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}
          <li>{{ item | escape | newline_to_br }}</li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- elsif bare -%}
      {{- f.value | escape | newline_to_br -}}
    {%- else -%}
      <p>{{ f.value | escape | newline_to_br }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Files. One `file_reference` can hold an image, a video or a document, and
    which one is only knowable from the drop Shopify returns - so each is
    sniffed by a property only it has: `sources` for video, `width` for an
    image. Testing `media_type` alone is not enough, because an image file
    reference returns an image drop with no media_type at all.

    A list is a stack rather than a <ul>: bullets beside a photograph read as a
    mistake.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'file_reference' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <div class="pdp-mf__media pdp-mf__media--stack">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}
          {%- if item.sources.size > 0 or item.media_type == 'video' -%}
            {{ item | video_tag: image_size: '1200x', autoplay: false, loop: true, muted: true, controls: true, preload: 'metadata' }}
          {%- elsif item.width > 0 -%}
            {{
              item
              | image_url: width: 1400
              | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', widths: '400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200, 1400', alt: item.alt
            }}
          {%- elsif item.url != blank -%}
            <p><a href="{{ item.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ item.alt | default: item.url }}</a></p>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      {%- assign file = f.value -%}
      {%- if file.sources.size > 0 or file.media_type == 'video' -%}
        <div class="pdp-mf__media">
          {{ file | video_tag: image_size: '1200x', autoplay: false, loop: true, muted: true, controls: true, preload: 'metadata' }}
        </div>
      {%- elsif file.width > 0 -%}
        <div class="pdp-mf__media">
          {{
            file
            | image_url: width: 1400
            | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', widths: '400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200, 1400', alt: file.alt
          }}
        </div>
      {%- elsif file.url != blank -%}
        <p><a href="{{ file.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ file.alt | default: file.url }}</a></p>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    A page reference is how a chart or a policy gets written once and reused on
    a hundred products. Its content is already HTML - tables included, which no
    text metafield type can carry - so it is output unescaped.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'page_reference' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      {%- for item in f.value -%}{{ item.content }}{%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ f.value.content }}
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- when 'url' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}
          <li><a href="{{ item }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ item }}</a></li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- else -%}
      <p><a href="{{ f.value }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ f.value }}</a></p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    A boolean is shown as a word because the rows that use one are statements a
    shopper reads - "Pre-shrunk: Yes" - not form state. `if f.value` rather
    than a string compare: the value is a real boolean.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'boolean' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Literal words rather than a translation key: `t` on a key no locale file
      defines returns the string "Translation missing: ...", which is not blank,
      so a `default` filter behind it never fires and the shopper reads the
      error. Adding the key to all fifty locale files is the fix if this page
      is ever translated.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p>
      {%- if f.value -%}Yes{%- else -%}No{%- endif -%}
    </p>

  {%- when 'number_integer' or 'number_decimal' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}<li>{{ item }}</li>{%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- else -%}
      <p>{{ f.value }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Measurements are objects, not numbers: {value, unit}. Printing the object
    gives the shopper a JSON blob, which is the single most common way a
    "dimension" metafield goes wrong on a live page.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'dimension' or 'weight' or 'volume' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}<li>{{ item.value }} {{ item.unit }}</li>{%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- else -%}
      <p>{{ f.value.value }} {{ f.value.unit }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- when 'rating' -%}
    <p>{{ f.value.rating }} / {{ f.value.scale_max }}</p>

  {%- comment -%}
    `money` rather than the raw amount, so the figure carries the shopper's
    currency and the market's formatting instead of a bare decimal.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'money' -%}
    <p>{{ f.value | money }}</p>

  {%- when 'date' or 'date_time' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}<li>{{ item | date: date_format }}</li>{%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- else -%}
      <p>{{ f.value | date: date_format }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    A hex string on its own tells a shopper nothing, so the chip carries the
    colour and the text carries the code for anyone who needs it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- when 'color' -%}
    <p class="pdp-mf__color">
      <span class="pdp-mf__swatch" style="background-color: {{ f.value }};"></span>
      <span>{{ f.value }}</span>
    </p>

  {%- when 'product_reference' or 'collection_reference' or 'variant_reference' -%}
    {%- if is_list -%}
      <ul class="pdp-mf__list">
        {%- for item in f.value -%}
          <li><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.title }}</a></li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- else -%}
      <p><a href="{{ f.value.url }}">{{ f.value.title }}</a></p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    An unknown or undefined type. A metafield created before its definition
    existed reports no type at all, and it is always a string in practice, so
    it is treated as one rather than dropped.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign fallback = f.value | default: f -%}
    {%- if fallback != blank -%}
      {%- if bare -%}
        {{- fallback | escape | newline_to_br -}}
      {%- else -%}
        <p>{{ fallback | escape | newline_to_br }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
{%- endcase -%}
